DragDropCtrl [SimTalk]
Designates a Method object of the object designated by <Path>.
Remarks
Plant Simulation calls the Method for the object designated by <Path>, which you drag onto another
object and drop there. The dropped object can be an object or text.
Note
You cannot use the Drag-and-drop Control in the Class Library by dragging and dropping an object
onto another object. The Drag-and-drop Control only works when you drag an object onto another
object in a Frame and drop it onto another object there.
Type
Attribute
Syntax
<Path>.DragDropCtrl:method
Parameter for point-oriented objects
The standard drag-and-drop control for point-oriented objects looks like this:
The parameter draggedObjects is an array of data type object that designates the path of the dragged and
dropped objects.
Parameters for length-oriented objects
The standard drag-and-drop control for length-oriented objects looks like this:
DragDropCtrl [SimTalk]
11-768 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

• The parameter draggedObjects is an array of data type object and designates the path of the dragged
and dropped objects.
• Plant Simulation sets the data types of the optional parameters for length-oriented objects when the
Drag-and-drop Control is called.
◦ For a length-oriented object the optional parameter dropPosition of data type length designates the
position starting at the beginning of the length-oriented object at which the object was dropped.
◦ For the TwoLaneTrack the optional parameter dropLane of data type string designates the lane on the
TwoLaneTrack on which the mouse button was released when dropping the object.
Drag-and-drop Control for Sensors
A Drag-and-drop Control for creating sensors on a length-oriented object and on a TwoLaneTrack might
look like this.
param droppedObjects: object[], dropPosition: any := void, lane: any := 
void 
// @,?: drop target
if lane = void
   if dropPosition /= void // length-oriented object
       ?.createSensor(dropPosition, "Length", void, true, false) 
   end
else                       // TwoLaneTrack
   if lane = "A"
       ?.A.createSensor(dropPosition, "Length", void, true, false)
   else
       ?.B.createSensor(dropPosition, "Length", void, true, false)
   end
end
Assignment Value
You can assign a value of data type method.
DragDropCtrl [SimTalk]
Plant Simulation Help 11-769
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

Examples
MyStation.DragDropCtrl := &myDragAndDrop
param draggedObjects: object[] 
// one-dimensional array with n objects 
// the size of which can change
var obj: object
// @,?: drop target
for var i := 1 to draggedObjects.dim
   obj := draggedObjects[i]
   // enter your source code here
   print i, ": ", draggedObjects[i] 
   // prints the dragged objects to the Console
next
See also
Drag-and-Drop Control
Use a Drag-and-Drop Control for Several Objects
DragDropCtrl [SimTalk]
11-770 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

FailCtrl [SimTalk]
Designates a Method object of the object designated by <Path>.
Remarks
Plant Simulation calls the Method whenever the Failed-state of the object designated by <Path> changes.
You can change the state in the dialog box of the object by selecting 
  or by assigning the value true
or false to the attribute Failed.
Within the control you can use the anonymous identifier question mark ? to access the object which
triggered the control.
Type
Attribute
Syntax
<Path>.FailCtrl:method
Parameters
Plant Simulation passes two optional parameters to the Method that is called:
• The optional parameter FailureStartEnd of data type boolean indicates if the failures starts (true) or
ends (false).
• The optional parameter FailureProfileName of data type string designates the name of the failure profile
that caused the failure.
Note
If the Fail Control declares both parameters, it will not only be called when the object changes to the
Failed-state or when the Failed-state ends, but also each time when a failure profile is failed or when
the failure is removed.
Let’s say failure profile 1 is already failed, and then you also fail failure profile 2 in addition, then the Fail
Control will still be called, although the object remains in the Failed-state.
Note
A Fail Control, which does not expect a parameter or only expects a single parameter, will only be called
if the state of the object actually changes, meaning it will not be called when another failure profile is
already failed. A Fail Control, which expects the name of the failure profile as the second parameter,
will, by contrast, always be called when a failure profile is failed or when the failure is removed.
FailCtrl [SimTalk]
Plant Simulation Help 11-771
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

Assignment Value
You can assign a value of data type method.
Example
.MUs.Transporter.FailCtrl := &myFailCtrl
if ?.failed 
    if ?.occupied and (NOT ?.cont.finished) 
        ?.imp.releaseExporters
    end
else
    if ?.occupied and (NOT ?.cont.finished) 
        ?.imp.import
    end
end
See also
Fail Control [material flow objects]
Failed [state - material flow objects]
Failed [check box] - material flow objects
Failed [check box] - material flow objects
FailCtrl [SimTalk]
11-772 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

InitCtrl [SimTalk] - general description
Designates a Method object of the object designated by <Path>.
Remarks
Plant Simulation calls the Method once at the beginning of the simulation run during the init phase. It is
called before the object designated by <Path> is initialized and before the init controls are being executed.
Within the Init Control you can, for example, initialize the Transporter with suitable values and assign
user-defined attributes or change the Workers to Create table of the WorkerPool for the next simulation
run. This cannot be accomplished in an init method as this method would be called too late in time.
You can also initialize attributes which affect event generation, for example the Availability. Here the Init
Controls are executed before events are computed. The normal init methods are executed after the initial
events have been computed.
Type
Attribute
Syntax
<Path>.InitCtrl:method
Assignment Value
You can assign a value of data type method.
Example
.MUs.Transporter.InitCtrl := &myInitCtrl
M1.setupFor("C",false)
M2.setupFor("B",false)
M3.setupFor("A",false)
M1._3D.getObject("SetupType").setGraphicMaterial([1], 
~.UserObjects.C._3D.MaterialDiffuseColor, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0)
M2._3D.getObject("SetupType").setGraphicMaterial([1], 
~.UserObjects.B._3D.MaterialDiffuseColor, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0)
M3._3D.getObject("SetupType").setGraphicMaterial([1], 
~.UserObjects.A._3D.MaterialDiffuseColor, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0)
InitCtrl [SimTalk] - general description
Plant Simulation Help 11-773
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

SimTalk
init [SimTalk] - predefined name
See also
Init Control [general description]
Init Control [Transporter]
Init Control [EventController]
Init Control [WorkerPool]
Workers to Create
InitCtrl [SimTalk] - general description
