SelectCtrl [SimTalk]
Designates a Method object of the object designated by <Path>.
Remarks
Plant Simulation calls the Method when you click the object designated by <Path> with the left mouse
button. Plant Simulation then does not select the object.
Instead, the control you entered has to take the appropriate actions, for example by opening the object, or
by confirming the selection with the attribute Selected, etc.
3D models also support the Select Control. Plant Simulation calls the Method when the user clicks the
mouse button. You can prevent calling the method by holding down the Ctrl key when clicking the mouse
button.
Type
Attribute
Syntax
<Path>.SelectCtrl:method
Assignment Value
You can assign a value of data type method.
Example
MyStation.SelectCtrl := .Tools.&mySelectCtrl
print @.Selected
@.Selected := true
See also
Select Control
_3D.Selected [SimTalk]
SelectCtrl [SimTalk]
11-792 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

UnplannedCtrl [SimTalk]
Designates a Method object of the object designated by <Path>.
Remarks
Plant Simulation calls the Method whenever the Unplanned state of the object designated by <Path>
changes, i.e., whether the object is not scheduled to work during the time, which you typed into the
ShiftCalendar or whether it is scheduled to work.
You can change the state in the dialog of the object by selecting an entry in the drop-down list Paused/
Planned/Unplanned 
  or by assigning another value to the attribute Unplanned.
Note
The ShiftCalendar uses the attribute Unplanned to designate the time period during which the object is
not planned to work during a shift.
Type
Attribute
Syntax
<Path>.UnplannedCtrl:method
Parameters
You can specify the following parameters:
• The parameter Duration of data type real sets the length of the shift pause. If the length of the shift
pause is unknown, Plant Simulation passes -1, as well as when the control is called at the end of the
shift pause.
• The parameter NewValue of data type boolean sets that controls will only be called if the ShiftCalendar
changes the state of the object.
In addition the object will not be put in the desired state. The Unplanned Control has to do this. The
desired value is passed in the parameter NewValue.
The part still can be processed in its entirety before the object changes to the state unplanned.
Within the control you can access the object with the anonymous identifier ?.
UnplannedCtrl [SimTalk]
Plant Simulation Help 11-793
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

Note
If the unplanned control has the parameters listed above, then the ShiftCalendar will not automatically
change the state unplanned. This is useful to complete processing of a part and then end the shift
manually by setting the attribute Unplanned of the object to true.
Compare this example:
param Duration: real, NewValue: boolean
if ?.Cont /= void
   wait ?.Cont.RemainingProcTime
end
?.Unplanned := NewValue
Note
If the Unplanned Control does not have any parameters, Plant Simulation always calls the Unplanned
Control when the state unplanned changes.
If the Unplanned Control has the parameters listed above, Plant Simulation only calls the Unplanned
Control when the state unplanned is changed by the ShiftCalendar. This prevents that the Unplanned
Control calls itself when the state unplanned is set manually.
The standard unplanned control as a user-defined attribute looks like this:
Assignment Value
You can assign a value of data type method.
Examples
MyStation.SelectCtrl := &myUnplannedCtrl
print "Frame unplanned: ", current.unplanned
MyStation.unplanned := current.unplanned
if current.unplanned 
   current.currIcon := "unplanned"
else
   if current.pause 
